{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on request be4fddb5: take your first recommendation. The\n\"replay corpus\" of Hugh's condition is the set of judgments that\nthe replay test histories reach.\n\n1. Owners: each test file under `packages/replay/test` that builds\n   a history through the Ledger of `fixtures.ts`, and the judge\n   owner in `packages/derive` for refusals and idempotency. Name\n   each owner and its entry count in the E1 note.\n2. Record each `judgeAct` call that those owners reach, with its\n   real runtime context. Record the `judgeActForReplay` calls as a\n   second list. Do not merge the two lists, and do not treat one\n   context as the other.\n3. The definition corpus rows are declarations and validations.\n   They are not judgments. Do not count them.\n4. Leave the proof and clock callbacks as they are.\n5. Produce the fixture once at the merge base after the combined\n   head lands, before the split. One row holds the owner, the case,\n   the call index, the SHA-256 of the canonical input and the\n   SHA-256 of the canonical answer. The test after the split\n   compares each row and prints the input and both answers on a\n   difference.\n6. Keep the fixture under the 40,960-byte limit for a committed\n   object. If it is larger, tell me its size before you commit it.\n\nThe registry of constructors is not required. If an owner cannot\nbe recorded without a production change, name it in the note as a\nboundary that the fixture does not cover.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}